[{"result":{"data":{"json":{"id":"1784","stub":{"id":"1784","section":"Wash-Pelfrey Road to Spring City","altname":"Wash-Pelfrey Road to Spring City<br>Alternate putin on Mossican Cr, unmarked log","river":"Piney River","difficulty":"IIItoIV","states":["USA-TNN"]},"primaryGaugeStatus":null,"pointOfInterests":[{"type":"rapid","description":"This is your landmark, for the Slide rapid that is coming up","id":"2390","location":{"latitude":"0","longitude":"0"},"name":"Begin of Slide Section","distance":"0","difficulty":null,"approximate":false,"photoID":"13529"},{"type":"rapid","description":"The first major rapid after Moccasin Creek","id":"2389","location":{"latitude":"0","longitude":"0"},"name":"Signal Falls","distance":"0","difficulty":null,"approximate":false,"photoID":"13528"},{"type":"rapid","description":"One of the last big ones, with more water it gets more of a bite","id":"2391","location":{"latitude":"0","longitude":"0"},"name":"Hungry Jack","distance":"0","difficulty":null,"approximate":false,"photoID":"13530"},{"type":"put-in","description":"The alternate putin, where Pine Creek Rd. crosses Moccasin Creek. Lat/long coords are approximate, from Google Maps.","id":"100929","location":{"latitude":"35.64958953857422","longitude":"-84.96739959716797"},"name":"Moccasin Creek putin","distance":"0","photoID":"0","difficulty":"N/A","approximate":true},{"type":"takeout","description":null,"id":"114568","location":{"latitude":"35.7053","longitude":"-84.8728"},"name":"Take Out","distance":"10.4","photoID":null,"difficulty":"N/A","approximate":false}],"alerts":[{"id":"101248032","title":null,"post_date":"2011-05-01 04:00:00+00","detail":"LOTS OF WOOD!!!\n\nOn April 27, 2011 a tornado ripped through Spring City, TN and up the Piney River valley. It crossed the the Piney about a dozen times. Don Ellis led us down on April 29. At the time we had no way of knowing the tornado had gone up the river. Above Moccasin Creek (the top 2 miles) there were 2 MAJOR log jambs. The second was the worst with a thick matting of trees a couple feet deep and 100 yards long laying over the Piney! From Moccasin Creek down, there was only one more river wide strainer consisting of a few trees.\n\nConsider everything above Moccasin unboatable. Everything below Moccasin will likely become unboatable as the stuff upstream breaks up and washes down.\n\nThe level was 2.5 feet.\n\n2.5 feet was a real treat. It could probably be run a little lower, but you'd knuckle a couple of spots.","user":{"name":"Jon Reed"}}],"photos":[{"id":"878757","ext":"jpeg","post_id":"879192","description":"This was our first sighting of wood.  As you can see on the right of the photo, the 4/27/11 tornado snapped LARGE pines and dropped them in/across the river bed.","caption":"First sign of wood","poi_name":"","subject":"","author":"","poi_id":null,"photo_date":null,"updated_at":null,"url":{"medium":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/medium/878757","large":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/big/878757"}},{"id":"1890","ext":"jpeg","post_id":"3774","description":"See the full-sized version of this picture by <a href=\"http://www.americanwhitewater.org/photos/?photoid=1208\">clicking here</a>.","caption":"End of the Slide","poi_name":"","subject":"Craig Rollins","author":"Kemper Begley","poi_id":null,"photo_date":null,"updated_at":null,"url":{"medium":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/medium/1890","large":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/big/1890"}},{"id":"1209","ext":"jpeg","post_id":"3774","description":"This is the last really difficult rapid on the Piney River. I should have been further to river left at this point. Oops! ","caption":"Last big one of the journey","poi_name":"Nosebone?","subject":"Kemper Begley","author":"Craig Rollins","poi_id":"100929","photo_date":null,"updated_at":null,"url":{"medium":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/medium/1209","large":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/big/1209"}},{"id":"14352","ext":"jpeg","post_id":"2676","description":"","caption":"Hungry Jack","poi_name":"","subject":"","author":"Paul B","poi_id":null,"photo_date":null,"updated_at":null,"url":{"medium":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/medium/14352","large":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/big/14352"}},{"id":"878758","ext":"jpeg","post_id":"879193","description":"It isn't just a couple of trees, but possibly hundreds dropped into and across the river.","caption":"Tornado damage to the Piney landscape","poi_name":null,"subject":null,"author":"","poi_id":null,"photo_date":null,"updated_at":null,"url":{"medium":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/medium/878758","large":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/big/878758"}},{"id":"14349","ext":"jpeg","post_id":"2676","description":"","caption":"Piney 1st drop","poi_name":"","subject":"Drew","author":"Paul B","poi_id":null,"photo_date":null,"updated_at":null,"url":{"medium":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/medium/14349","large":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/big/14349"}},{"id":"14350","ext":"jpeg","post_id":"2676","description":"","caption":"Piney 1st drop","poi_name":"1st drop","subject":"Kemper","author":"Paul B","poi_id":null,"photo_date":null,"updated_at":null,"url":{"medium":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/medium/14350","large":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/big/14350"}},{"id":"14351","ext":"jpeg","post_id":"2676","description":"","caption":"Piney scene above Hungry Jack","poi_name":"","subject":"","author":"Paul B","poi_id":null,"photo_date":null,"updated_at":null,"url":{"medium":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/medium/14351","large":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/big/14351"}},{"id":"14353","ext":"jpeg","post_id":"2676","description":"","caption":"Piney ledge","poi_name":"","subject":"Drew","author":"Paul B","poi_id":null,"photo_date":null,"updated_at":null,"url":{"medium":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/medium/14353","large":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/big/14353"}},{"id":"6302","ext":"jpeg","post_id":"3775","description":"","caption":"Piney River","poi_name":"?","subject":"Craig Rollins","author":"Kemper Begley","poi_id":null,"photo_date":null,"updated_at":null,"url":{"medium":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/medium/6302","large":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/big/6302"}},{"id":"6301","ext":"jpeg","post_id":"3775","description":"","caption":"Sentinel Falls","poi_name":"Sentinel Falls","subject":"Kemper Begley","author":"Paul Butler","poi_id":null,"photo_date":null,"updated_at":null,"url":{"medium":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/medium/6301","large":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/big/6301"}},{"id":"6303","ext":"jpeg","post_id":"3775","description":"A sample of the things that a paddler on the Piney River will see","caption":"Piney River Scenery","poi_name":"?","subject":"Craig Rollins (orange boat) Paul Butler (red boat)","author":"Kemper Begley","poi_id":null,"photo_date":null,"updated_at":null,"url":{"medium":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/medium/6303","large":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/big/6303"}},{"id":"2600","ext":"jpeg","post_id":"4944","description":"This is the drop on the run","caption":"Piney river","poi_name":"First Drop","subject":"Mark H","author":"Wade Harrison","poi_id":null,"photo_date":null,"updated_at":null,"url":{"medium":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/medium/2600","large":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/big/2600"}},{"id":"892280","ext":"jpg","post_id":"896178","description":null,"caption":" on Piney River @Wash-Pelfrey Road to Spring City","poi_name":null,"subject":null,"author":null,"poi_id":null,"photo_date":null,"updated_at":null,"url":{"medium":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/medium/892280","large":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/big/892280"}},{"id":"1208","ext":"jpeg","post_id":"3774","description":"This is the drop at the end of a long, fast slide on Piney River.","caption":"End of the Slide","poi_name":"Unknown","subject":"Craig Rollins","author":"Kemper Begley","poi_id":"100929","photo_date":null,"updated_at":null,"url":{"medium":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/medium/1208","large":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/big/1208"}}],"detail":{"id":"1784","permitUrl":null,"permitInfo":null,"description":"Support the Stream Beam group for the upkeep of their gauge at the takeout bridge.\n\nFlow gauge:\n\n<https://www.streambeam.net/>\n\n12 miles seems more likely for the length of this run (Eddlemon's book)\n\nRob Martin:\n\n'This is not the creek to be on if you're out of shape. It's not a good creek to test your skills on.\n\n'There is one section that is class IV-IV+; the rest is class II-III (not hair). But this run is\n\nlong, physically demanding and remote. Bring some food to replenish your energy levels. But it is a beautiful place to play.'\n\n**High Water comment from Geoff Kohl 06-13-2002 at 8ft**\n\nAt about 8 feet, this river has some rapids that are class V and last for lengths of a football field or more. At this level, there are a couple drops that get what I would consider possible terminal holes. What's more, these nasty drops are in the middle of pure screaming hair, often giving paddlers the choice of one eddy before them. Having paddled this at that level, I would not recommend this level for anyone who doesn't know the run fairly well. We didn't know what was around some corners and were lucky enough to stop before we met a few huge holes. In addition, this is not for the faint of heart, as it's possible you will bomb down a series of huge haystacks playing slalom with holes that want to make swimmers, with no foreseeable end to the rapid in sight. Portages lead up through thick forests and over huge chunks of sandstone. At this level, it would compare to running Pillow Rock on the Gauley blind without the nice pool at the bottom, plus with bigger, nastier holes. I'd do it again at this level, but I know quite a few who would not, and they are probably better men because of that. *(Note: this refers to the old gauge, before the takeout bridge was rebuilt. The corresponding level from the current gauge is unknown.)*\n\nEd Note: The highest I know of was at 10 ft in the Winter of 1993, and was a handful all the way to the take-out. With water levels this high, and after our run I recommend [Little Soddy](http://www.americanwhitewater.org/content/River/view/river-detail/4271).\n\nJohn Tansil shared:\n\n'Some early history - The Piney River was first paddled in 1972 (late 71?) by Martin Begun, other members of the East Tennessee White Water Club, and guest boaters, mostly from the upper Midwest, who traditionally came down to paddle with ETWWC on winter/spring holidays. Friends from that initial trip related details of the first descent to me on my first Piney run in spring 1973.\n\n'Martin B scouted the whole run by foot before paddling it. The first descent was an 'epic' with high water, a big group of 17 (starting out), and lots of carnage. They were in composite K1s or C1s, mostly homemade since both groups had active boatbuilding programs. Kenny Cooper broke his boat in one of the drops and had to walk out. 'Hungry Jack' was (I think?) named for Jack Wright after a trashing. Don Jared painted the first gauge on the bridge at the take-out. The Piney quickly became a favorite run but ETWWC didn't publicize it.\n\n'For several years after, the only groups on the Piney were people from the first runs and a few others. When I paddled it in spring 1975, we were again the only group on the river even though none of the other creeks further south on Walden Ridge had been discovered. Over the next 10 years, plastic boats and the warm water of the Ocoee contributed to a sharp rise in both the skill and number of southeastern paddlers.\n\n'Two guidebooks on Tennessee whitewater, one by Mayfield and another by Sehlinger, helped to popularize the Piney during the early 80s. When I paddled the Piney in 1987 with Lee Belknap and others, it was fairly well-known. The initial rating of class IV-V was as much a statement about boat design and boating skills at the time as it was about the difficulty of the run. The Piney shuttle map is on the front cover of the Tennessee Gazetteer (1989 DeLorme version) except that most is obscured by a logo. The Piney was discovered at about the same time as the [Caney Fork](http://americanwhitewater.org/content/River/view/river-detail/1727/)/ [Bee Creek](http://americanwhitewater.org/content/River/view/river-detail/1717/) combo and these three were the first Plateau creek runs other than Obed tributaries. Both the Piney and Caney Fork are true classics that should be enjoyed by everyone with the skill to paddle them.'\n\nAnyone having more details regarding this run is encouraged to either provide them (via the 'Add a comment' button, which should appear for registered, logged-in users of this site) or (AW members only) sign up as a StreamTeam Volunteer to 'adopt' this section of river.","averageGradient":"73","maxGradient":"120","length":"15.1","correlations":[],"pointOfInterestIDs":["2390","2389","2391","100929","114568"],"geometry":{"type":"LineString","coordinates":[[-84.992976,35.611255],[-84.992935,35.611392],[-84.992875,35.611579],[-84.992855,35.611969],[-84.992775,35.612184],[-84.992721,35.612381],[-84.992505,35.612321],[-84.99223,35.612244],[-84.99196,35.612118],[-84.991772,35.611986],[-84.991651,35.611871],[-84.991583,35.611717],[-84.991428,35.611569],[-84.99093,35.611135],[-84.990782,35.61108],[-84.990641,35.611086],[-84.990419,35.611163],[-84.990264,35.611262],[-84.99021,35.611327],[-84.99019,35.611415],[-84.990184,35.611553],[-84.990204,35.611564],[-84.990224,35.611608],[-84.990271,3